\f0\fs24 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs26 But where, indeed?\
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs26 \
Not in the clay.\
Not in the glaze.\
Not in the gentle wavering line where the potter's wheel hesitated -\
that little quiver that tells you he had a stone in his shoe.\
\
All of these are revealed to a certain reader of the vase, and begin to tell us a story before the fingers make contact.\
\
Then, it must be that the glyphs are part of the story of the vase, and, they are understood by the exploration of its surface.\
\
What work then to echo triteness.  And what a waste of our effort, we think as problem of what is changed as we read the vase momentarily disappears.\
\
The charcoal burner brings us tea, even after we've sent away the girl wearing hidden bells. Unseen and her bare neck smelling of gum. Tonight I sleep alone in a hive of humming bees. If I reach out, I can just feel it even when the bell is silent, that spot where it hides.\
\
The invisible, rejected when immanent, in absence, comes forth and we can nearly imagine what color is, from the tension brought forth by the unseen.\
\
That call that never goes unanswered, given time, we find in both, even if relief is only found sometimes in tea.\
\
Some point to how unbalanced I am, that every day I make the poor scribe stop and begin again, only to reveal another way to describe how things want to be like unto like, so much as they remain themselves.\
\
This then, is how we read the vase with our hands, and there's no need to close the circle, for it feels certain that a very law of the universe demands what there be written, and, that we should know it.\
We are reminded then, that some things seem eternal.\
\
Perhaps the answer then is to simply accept that this is how it is. A position most brave and cowardly at once.\
\

\f2\i A woman's glint reflected in a man's eye was taken by her to be the entire world and in she was drawn.\
\
